<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128916" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202916/HVN3NLsW0AApS9U.jpeg" alt width="1200" height="1600" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202916/HVN3NLsW0AApS9U.jpeg 1200w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202916/HVN3NLsW0AApS9U-270x360.jpeg 270w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202916/HVN3NLsW0AApS9U-768x1024.jpeg 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202916/HVN3NLsW0AApS9U-180x240.jpeg 180w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202916/HVN3NLsW0AApS9U-1152x1536.jpeg 1152w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px"></p>
<p align="right"><small><em><a href="https://x.com/MehmetNuriErsoy/status/2113163727146983643?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E2113163727146983643%7Ctwgr%5E6a558ccc0847228a6f667012906f768132a0831a%7Ctwcon%5Es1_&amp;ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.smithsonianmag.com%2Fsmart-news%2Fcrafted-from-bone-this-2800-year-old-flute-unearthed-in-troy-may-have-been-played-around-the-time-that-homer-composed-the-iliad-and-the-odyssey-180989792%2F">Image by Mehmet Nuri Ersoy, via Twitter</a></em></small></p>
<p><a href="https://www.openculture.com/2017/08/9-hours-of-hans-zimmer-soundtracks.html">The prolific Hans Zimmer</a> scored Christopher Nolan’s films over the dozen years from <em>Batman Begins&nbsp;</em>through <em>Dunkirk</em>. From 2020’s&nbsp;<em>Tenet&nbsp;</em>onward, Nolan has employed the younger Swedish composer <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludwig_G%C3%B6ransson">Ludwig Göransson</a>.&nbsp;Just what difference this has made in the resulting pictures is a matter best left to the scrutiny of the most obsessive fans, but any viewer can hear how much Zimmer and Göransson differ stylistically. This past summer, the <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2_-XPMYQmXc">siren song</a> included on the soundtrack of <em>The</em> <em>Odyssey</em>&nbsp;featured one of the latter’s particularly notable choices: the use of an aulos, a double-reeded wind instrument much played back in ancient Greece.</p>
<p>Though Göransson didn’t have a genuinely ancient aulos on hand, of course, the sound produced by the replica proved evocative enough. But just this year, as it happens,&nbsp;a bone flute&nbsp;actually dating from the period in which Homer wrote <em>The Odyssey</em>&nbsp;was unearthed from the archaeological site of ancient Troy in modern-day&nbsp;northwestern Turkey.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>As <a href="https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/crafted-from-bone-this-2800-year-old-flute-unearthed-in-troy-may-have-been-played-around-the-time-that-homer-composed-the-iliad-and-the-odyssey-180989792/">Smithsonian.com’s Meilan Solly writes</a>, the past 160 years of digging there have produced “a pair of gold diadems, more than 100 two-handled wine goblets, and arrowheads and weapons dated to the Late Bronze Age, among other artifacts.” This 2,800-year-old flute isn’t just the first musical instrument discovered at the site, but also further evidence that Troy wasn’t the ghost town it was once assumed to be in the eighth century BC.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128917" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202918/HVN3M7MWYAAo6Rm.jpeg" alt width="1200" height="1600" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202918/HVN3M7MWYAAo6Rm.jpeg 1200w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202918/HVN3M7MWYAAo6Rm-270x360.jpeg 270w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202918/HVN3M7MWYAAo6Rm-768x1024.jpeg 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202918/HVN3M7MWYAAo6Rm-180x240.jpeg 180w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/11202918/HVN3M7MWYAAo6Rm-1152x1536.jpeg 1152w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px"></p>
<p>As always when dealing with ancient artifacts — and especially ones, as these pictures reveal, in a less-than-whole state — we should be careful with our terms. “Calling the object a flute is the excavators’ choice of word, and the type remains open,” <a href="https://www.ancient-origins.net/news-history-archaeology/troy-bone-flute-first-instrument-homer-00103293">says Ancient Origins</a>. While this new discovery could potentially be a real Greek aulos, “without hole counts or a published drawing, matching the Troy find to that design is premature.” Archaeologists have unearthed similar instruments elsewhere, dating from different periods, but never before has one emerged from the buried remains of a city so rich in legendary associations. To those in search of a deeper understanding of antiquity, the site continues to sound a siren song of its own.</p>
